  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nMarine layer low clouds are much slower to develop this morning\nwith only patchy coverage near the San Diego County coast. Clouds\nshould slowly fill in over the coastal areas and western valleys\nby sunrise. Otherwise a band of high clouds is moving northeast\nover Baja into Imperial County with clear skies elsewhere. \n\nA slow warming trend begins today as a broad upper level high over\nthe southern US begins to expand westward into So Cal. High\ntemperatures today will be near to slightly below normal -\ngenerally mid to upper 70s along the coast, mid 80s for the\nwestern valleys, low to mid 90s for the inland valleys, upper 70s\nto mid 80s in the mountains, mid to upper 90s in the high desert,\nand 105-108 in the low desert. Some residual mid-level moisture\nremains, but with drying near 850 mb and the subsidence inversion\nstrengthening, any convection over the mountains will be high-\nbased and rather shallow. Can't discount a few showers over the\nhigher peaks of the San Bernardino Mountains, where there is a 15%\nchance of showers/thunderstorms this afternoon.\n\nThe warming trend continues into Thursday when high temperatures\nclimb to 3 to 7 degrees above normal. For Wednesday through Friday,\nthere is around a 15-20% chance of high temperatures of 105F or \nmore over the interior portions of the Inland Empire (Lake \nElsinore to Hemet), and a 45-65% chance of high temperatures of \n110F or more in the low desert. Widespread moderate HeatRisk is \nforecast for the valleys, lower elevation mountains, and deserts \nWednesday through Friday, with areas of high HeatRisk in the low \ndesert. Low elevation dew points/humidity remain elevated through\nthe week as well, keeping minimum temperatures several degrees\nabove normal. Basically another hot and muggy week is in store.\nThe marine layer will become more shallow with areas of night and\nmorning low clouds and fog becoming confined to the coastal areas\nand far western valleys.\n\nThursday through Saturday, the upper level high shifts towards the\nFour Corners region, allowing for additional monsoonal moisture to\ntrack into CA and slow cooling to occur. There remains subtle \nspread in the ensembles with respect to the position of the high \nand the orientation of the ridge axis, which will determine how \nmuch moisture and instability we see. For now there is at least a \nslight (15-20%) chance of showers and thunderstorms over the \nmountains and high desert each afternoon Thursday through \nSaturday, with lower chances on Sunday as the upper high shifts \neast and moisture begins to decrease.",
